```gleam
generated
```
:::

::: g-note
*Anyone can write anything.* The typed `Note` record is a place where you decode incoming data, not a schema the server enforces — an older version of the app, or a stale copy, could put any string into that field. So `from_register` doesn't have an error case to forget about: a value that fails to decode becomes a visible placeholder card instead. A card you can see and recognize as broken beats a crash that takes the whole board down.
:::

{data-component="field-note-ref" data-practice="stamp-schema"}
:::
:::

::: g-block
## Adding a note is one map write

Mint an id, encode the note, write it to the map. The nonce — a small random number added just to keep ids unique — is there because two notes from one author can land in the same millisecond.

{data-snippet="guide-notes-add-note"}
```gleam
generated
```

`board` keeps the UI and the tests on the exact same write function, not two separate copies of the same logic. If a test reimplemented the write instead of calling it, all that would prove is that the reimplementation agrees with itself. Step 06 depends on this.

